176
00:18:36,331 --> 00:18:48,260
[SPEAKER_01]: For as long as you feel a need exactly to defend that view, it means there is an assumption of views there, which would not be present in somebody who has understood the nature of assumption, which is somebody who has found the middle way.

177
00:18:49,441 --> 00:19:02,250
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's a criteria that doesn't matter how much it works for you, if you still kind of... Obviously, I'm not saying that

178
00:19:03,162 --> 00:19:07,303
[SPEAKER_01]: You kind of abandon the recognition that some things are right and some things are wrong.

179
00:19:07,563 --> 00:19:08,384
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not what it means.

180
00:19:08,484 --> 00:19:17,467
[SPEAKER_01]: It means, emotionally, if you feel a need to defend what you regard as the middle way, then that's that's not the middle way.

181
00:19:17,787 --> 00:19:20,608
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it should be... like, it means you still doubt it.

182
00:19:21,368 --> 00:19:28,050
[SPEAKER_01]: It means, like, the the statements of another person are kind of internally shaking you to the point where

183
00:19:28,870 --> 00:19:33,853
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't help but to try to justify why this thing is right.

184
00:19:33,993 --> 00:19:41,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Your whole sense of self, or let's say personality view, can be built up even more with your

185
00:19:42,432 --> 00:19:55,601
[SPEAKER_00]: Religious or spiritual practices, making whole identity out of that, somebody questioning those practices, and then you feel, in the core of your being, challenged in what you take yourself to be.

186
00:19:55,701 --> 00:19:58,663
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a rather serious sign that you're having the whole thing backwards.

187
00:19:58,823 --> 00:20:08,170
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because, again, people may confuse, supposedly not clinging to any views, which is another view, that you would be upset if, like,

188
00:20:09,152 --> 00:20:16,213
[SPEAKER_01]: When you hold to the view, that you shouldn't cling to any views, you see people clinging to their views, and you get upset with them.

189
00:20:16,313 --> 00:20:17,374
[SPEAKER_01]: And you start telling them off.

190
00:20:17,514 --> 00:20:19,354
[SPEAKER_01]: That's because you're clinging to your view right there.

191
00:20:20,074 --> 00:20:21,134
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eh...

192
00:20:22,695 --> 00:20:30,676
[SPEAKER_01]: But they fail to see, for example, all the Buddha spent time doing was saying, this is right, this is wrong, this is right, this is wrong.

193
00:20:31,836 --> 00:20:35,177
[SPEAKER_01]: And he was not clinging to his views at all.

194
00:20:35,617 --> 00:20:35,877
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

195
00:20:37,228 --> 00:20:42,813
[SPEAKER_01]: People need to look for deeper criteria than just statements that you make.

196
00:20:43,533 --> 00:20:45,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Because clearly that was not the case.

197
00:20:45,515 --> 00:20:54,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the Buddha did not manifest his non-clinging through making kind of open statements about maybe other people are right.

198
00:20:54,422 --> 00:20:55,243
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not the one.

199
00:20:55,643 --> 00:20:56,424
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not the only one.

200
00:20:57,005 --> 00:20:57,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Quite the contrary.

201
00:20:58,045 --> 00:20:59,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, or for some people...

202
00:20:59,647 --> 00:21:05,412
[SPEAKER_01]: He even said like, whoever does not see things in this way, I say that they are not a true renunciant.

203
00:21:06,473 --> 00:21:07,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you went that far.

204
00:21:08,134 --> 00:21:10,316
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's not what clinging to views is.

205
00:21:11,476 --> 00:21:13,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

206
00:21:13,858 --> 00:21:15,139
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is the view...

207
00:21:15,459 --> 00:21:19,462
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there are the views that are right and that are wrong from the point of view of dhamma.

208
00:21:19,882 --> 00:21:21,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Factually, that's one thing.

209
00:21:21,583 --> 00:21:27,247
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, how one, let's say, holds, quote-unquote, that internally, that's another thing.

210
00:21:27,887 --> 00:21:28,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

211
00:21:28,448 --> 00:21:34,972
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, similarly, there is the observance of, let's say, precepts, and in a wider context, there is virtue.

212
00:21:35,700 --> 00:21:47,703
[SPEAKER_00]: But how one is regarding that internally, that will be the factor that determines whether somebody falls into the assumption of... or the view of Sīla Bhāṭṭhapāramāsa, or not.

213
00:21:48,564 --> 00:22:00,567
[SPEAKER_01]: You could... about the views, you could basically say, as opposed to thinking that the right view is another view that you take on, like, I was following this other thing, now I just follow this one.

214
00:22:00,687 --> 00:22:04,468
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like, the right view is the understanding of all views.

215
00:22:05,079 --> 00:22:07,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's the overcoming of the assumption of views.

216
00:22:08,661 --> 00:22:12,944
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the right view is the view that understands the nature of views.

217
00:22:13,985 --> 00:22:20,950
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it makes you kind of incapable to cling to it, because it's not on the same level as all the other views.

218
00:22:21,030 --> 00:22:26,473
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not operating on the same kind of, I choose to believe this, as opposed to the other thing.

219
00:22:26,954 --> 00:22:33,298
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, I understand what it is that would make me believe in anything.

220
00:22:34,140 --> 00:22:37,982
[SPEAKER_01]: And I see that as... not mine, so to say.

221
00:22:38,082 --> 00:22:40,543
[SPEAKER_01]: I see that as not worth holding on to.

222
00:22:41,164 --> 00:22:42,244
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the right view is.

223
00:22:42,324 --> 00:22:56,251
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if others try to criticize that, anything that others could be criticizing would be a specific view, not the understanding of views.

224
00:22:56,271 --> 00:22:56,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

225
00:22:56,532 --> 00:23:00,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, who can criticize, who can say that understanding views is bad?

226
00:23:00,173 --> 00:23:00,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody can say that.

227
00:23:01,034 --> 00:23:01,214
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

228
00:23:01,715 --> 00:23:08,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever they talk about, by necessity, won't be the actual... when they're criticizing it, won't be the right view.

229
00:23:09,780 --> 00:23:18,745
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you... again, if you feel offended, that's because what is being targeted is not the right view, and you're getting offended on account of it.

230
00:23:18,765 --> 00:23:19,826
[SPEAKER_01]: It means you're holding on to it.

231
00:23:20,223 --> 00:23:20,983
[SPEAKER_01]: To the wrong view.

232
00:23:21,003 --> 00:23:21,584
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

233
00:23:22,064 --> 00:23:22,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, to any view.

234
00:23:22,784 --> 00:23:27,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, holding to views is wrong in and of itself.

235
00:23:27,327 --> 00:23:30,008
[SPEAKER_01]: The right view is not something that you can hold on to.

236
00:23:30,268 --> 00:23:30,908
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the difference.

237
00:23:31,109 --> 00:23:31,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's the contradiction.

238
00:23:31,989 --> 00:23:32,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

239
00:23:32,269 --> 00:23:32,549
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

240
00:23:33,350 --> 00:23:33,710
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

241
00:23:36,433 --> 00:23:38,554
[SPEAKER_01]: The same with the virtue.

242
00:23:38,954 --> 00:23:48,018
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that, you know, when one overcomes attachment to virtue practices, one stops being virtuous.

243
00:23:48,918 --> 00:24:00,103
[SPEAKER_02]: It's more like... because, as the Buddha said, the actual practice of virtue is seeing the danger in the slightest thought.

244
00:24:01,020 --> 00:24:04,361
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not on the level of precept... specific precepts.

245
00:24:04,901 --> 00:24:08,502
[SPEAKER_01]: You keep the specific precepts so as to... because... as kind of guidelines.

246
00:24:09,102 --> 00:24:12,523
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there are certain things that can't... can never be done without them being false.

247
00:24:13,383 --> 00:24:27,827
[SPEAKER_01]: But, it shouldn't be like... it should... you should come to the point where you don't feel like being forced to not do something, being forced to give up one of your practices,

248
00:24:29,204 --> 00:24:31,946
[SPEAKER_01]: would feel like you're giving up the dhamma.

249
00:24:32,747 --> 00:24:37,130
[SPEAKER_01]: If it feels like that, then that's still something that you're holding on to.

250
00:24:37,710 --> 00:24:56,884
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the the the virtue, as we said in the last talk, the virtue in practice is made right, not by what you're externally doing, because that's kind of remains the same from puthujjana to arahant, just in the case of monks, keep the patimokkha, keep the eight precepts, relate people, and so on.

251
00:24:56,924 --> 00:24:57,645
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't change.

252
00:24:58,378 --> 00:25:13,871
[SPEAKER_02]: It's internally the way of relating to it, so that if you weren't able to keep any of this, for x or y reason, you would still not do anything unwholesome on the level of intentions.

253
00:25:13,891 --> 00:25:14,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

254
00:25:14,271 --> 00:25:27,662
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you were to forget, let's say, if you were to forget about everything you learned, you forget about the precepts, you don't know anything about the dhamma, if there is real understanding,

255
00:25:28,725 --> 00:25:34,631
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing the danger in the slightest fault, not engaging with unwholesome intentions by body and speech, that would remain.

256
00:25:36,033 --> 00:25:45,863
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's another way of testing, like, am I holding on to virtue and duty, which means self-mortification implicitly,

257
00:25:46,803 --> 00:25:58,027
[SPEAKER_02]: Would be, if I were to get, you know, lose my memory completely, if I just get Alzheimer's or something, will I... do I feel fear?

258
00:25:58,887 --> 00:26:04,809
[SPEAKER_02]: Any kind of trepidation that maybe this stuff that I've been developing will go...

259
00:26:05,009 --> 00:26:05,569
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll lose it.

260
00:26:07,210 --> 00:26:13,332
[SPEAKER_02]: If that's the case, then it's like losing those memories, basically.

261
00:26:14,769 --> 00:26:16,290
[SPEAKER_02]: What else is it?

262
00:26:16,330 --> 00:26:21,391
[SPEAKER_02]: Your practice is dependent on these memories, dependent on these things that could go away.

263
00:26:22,632 --> 00:26:24,612
[SPEAKER_02]: Just, you hit your head and you forget everything.

264
00:26:25,072 --> 00:26:26,793
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's just content, basically.

265
00:26:26,813 --> 00:26:27,033
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

266
00:26:28,053 --> 00:26:29,114
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that, that, exactly.

267
00:26:29,134 --> 00:26:34,876
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's a... for as long as it's that case, for as long as it's like that, it's depending, dependent on the content.

268
00:26:36,096 --> 00:26:37,476
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's exactly what you don't want.

269
00:26:37,536 --> 00:26:42,258
[SPEAKER_02]: As long as it's dependent on the content, it's not, it's not understanding.

270
00:26:43,090 --> 00:26:50,732
[SPEAKER_02]: As we said before, if it's a particular view that you can hold on to, it can't be the understanding of all views.

271
00:26:51,072 --> 00:26:54,632
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the understanding of all views is not a content, it's an understanding of content.

272
00:26:55,353 --> 00:26:59,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's a small background rather than... or context rather than content.

273
00:26:59,273 --> 00:26:59,453
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

274
00:26:59,954 --> 00:27:05,335
[SPEAKER_02]: So it's... so virtue should be a context.

275
00:27:05,775 --> 00:27:06,875
[SPEAKER_02]: A context, that's right.

276
00:27:08,225 --> 00:27:17,571
[SPEAKER_02]: It dictates your actions, but not because you feel like you have to be doing these specific actions, but because the content in itself is the determinant.

277
00:27:18,832 --> 00:27:19,432
[SPEAKER_02]: Of the context.

278
00:27:20,112 --> 00:27:22,794
[SPEAKER_02]: Sorry, yes, the context in itself is the determinant.

279
00:27:22,834 --> 00:27:23,234
[SPEAKER_02]: That's right.

280
00:27:23,314 --> 00:27:24,575
[SPEAKER_02]: Of what's right and what is wrong.

281
00:27:25,035 --> 00:27:28,177
[SPEAKER_02]: But, like, ordinary person starts the other way around.

282
00:27:28,277 --> 00:27:28,458
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

283
00:27:28,758 --> 00:27:31,800
[SPEAKER_02]: They try to do the particular stuff,

284
00:27:32,838 --> 00:27:41,044
[SPEAKER_02]: So as to reach the context, so as to reach the kind of wholesome and unwholesome, which is always on the level of the context.

285
00:27:41,345 --> 00:27:41,985
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

286
00:27:42,005 --> 00:27:47,189
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's like inverting the order, seeing that it should be the other way around.

287
00:27:47,630 --> 00:28:00,840
[SPEAKER_02]: So you can be doing the same specific actions, but if the context is wrong, then it doesn't matter how exactly to the letter it is, according to the precepts or whatever,

288
00:28:01,988 --> 00:28:03,549
[SPEAKER_02]: It is wrong because the context is wrong.

289
00:28:03,869 --> 00:28:04,089
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

290
00:28:04,270 --> 00:28:10,694
[SPEAKER_00]: And we have said earlier that intentions behind one's actions, this is what we're now referring to as context.

291
00:28:10,894 --> 00:28:11,094
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

292
00:28:12,335 --> 00:28:16,658
[SPEAKER_02]: So, those intentions as well, that's it.

293
00:28:16,738 --> 00:28:23,522
[SPEAKER_02]: That's also kind of where the right, the middle way would be recognized, as we were saying before.

294
00:28:24,303 --> 00:28:29,146
[SPEAKER_02]: If your practice depends on, if your practice is based on

295
00:28:29,832 --> 00:28:51,727
[SPEAKER_02]: Recognizing your intentions behind your actions and then abandoning certain intentions that you recognize are wrong, that should be... if you're truly doing that, that should lead you, as opposed to just kind of taking something on and running with it, like, okay, this is it and I keep doing it.

296
00:28:51,847 --> 00:28:52,107
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

297
00:28:53,328 --> 00:28:59,512
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're really doing that, questioning your intentions behind your actions, you would... it would be impossible for you not to recognize

298
00:29:00,235 --> 00:29:11,282
[SPEAKER_02]: The craving, the assumptions, the emotional dependence that is there on account of this meditation practice or whatever that you're doing.

299
00:29:11,923 --> 00:29:25,332
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if your practice is not revealing you those things, is not revealing you why is it that you're doing this at all,

300
00:29:28,137 --> 00:29:32,439
[SPEAKER_02]: How can you expect that it's going to review you what greed, aversion, and delusion actually are?

301
00:29:32,779 --> 00:29:34,720
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, so that has got to be the criteria.

302
00:29:34,760 --> 00:29:34,920
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

303
00:29:35,120 --> 00:29:35,300
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

304
00:29:35,560 --> 00:29:36,301
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this, this...

305
00:29:36,461 --> 00:29:38,221
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, I'm doing this, I'm doing that.

306
00:29:39,282 --> 00:29:46,025
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't argue on the basis of the content, because people might read the suttas and say, well, no, this is exactly what I'm doing.

307
00:29:46,445 --> 00:29:47,226
[SPEAKER_02]: The sutta says this.

308
00:29:48,006 --> 00:29:49,407
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

309
00:29:49,427 --> 00:29:54,409
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it is... what kind of makes it at least closer to be right or wrong is,

310
00:29:55,060 --> 00:30:01,022
[SPEAKER_02]: Is this actually revealing you these underlying intentions that you have, that you would otherwise have been taking for granted?

311
00:30:01,323 --> 00:30:01,563
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

312
00:30:01,963 --> 00:30:14,008
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's kind of, eh... digging, digging, delving with the knife, as the Buddha said, as opposed to what people usually assume that through a focusing practice, I'm delving closer into these sensations or something.

313
00:30:14,608 --> 00:30:16,629
[SPEAKER_02]: Delving with the knife is in the opposite direction.

314
00:30:16,809 --> 00:30:17,049
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

315
00:30:17,349 --> 00:30:19,810
[SPEAKER_02]: It's delving into these...

316
00:30:19,850 --> 00:30:20,830
[SPEAKER_02]: So, why am I doing this?

317
00:30:20,930 --> 00:30:23,612
[SPEAKER_02]: Where, what are, what is the thing that's motivating this?

318
00:30:23,672 --> 00:30:24,632
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's the kind of

319
00:30:25,547 --> 00:30:40,479
[SPEAKER_02]: If you want to hold on to a certain virtue and practice, that's basically the closest thing, that if you do it rightly, it will be taking you in the right direction, at least.

320
00:30:40,880 --> 00:30:41,720
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's the other thing.

321
00:30:41,740 --> 00:30:45,243
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't choose not to hold on to it, as long as you don't have the right view.

322
00:30:45,423 --> 00:30:49,287
[SPEAKER_02]: So you have to choose to hold on to the thing that's the least likely to be wrong.

323
00:30:49,527 --> 00:30:52,089
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, until that disappears as well.

324
00:30:52,209 --> 00:30:53,250
[SPEAKER_02]: And during that,

325
00:30:53,993 --> 00:31:11,647
[SPEAKER_02]: In this endeavor, so to say, you have to be telling yourself, no matter how much sense it makes, that there has to be some way in which I am still either indulging the aggregates, in the most general sense, or denying them, trying to get rid of them.

326
00:31:11,687 --> 00:31:13,249
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's what kind of keeps you...

327
00:31:14,510 --> 00:31:15,190
[SPEAKER_02]: It keeps you...

328
00:31:17,232 --> 00:31:18,713
[SPEAKER_02]: It forces you to keep refining it.

329
00:31:18,853 --> 00:31:20,655
[SPEAKER_02]: It forces you to keep looking further.

330
00:31:21,549 --> 00:31:26,692
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you're uncovering these intentions, because it's a constant process of refinement.

331
00:31:27,152 --> 00:31:36,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is basically, let's say, thinking or pondering or considering, right here and now, based on one's concrete situation.

332
00:31:36,757 --> 00:31:42,600
[SPEAKER_00]: So that cannot actually be mechanical or habitual, in the sense that methods and techniques usually are like that.

333
00:31:43,088 --> 00:31:52,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Regardless of how I feel, basically, regardless of this concrete situation, I'm going to do my practice, which is for me always the same.

334
00:31:52,530 --> 00:31:57,851
[SPEAKER_00]: That is another way in which you can see that it is not going right.

335
00:31:58,452 --> 00:32:06,754
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to precisely take into account this concrete situation, which is constituted by feeling, perception, mood, and all the rest of it.

336
00:32:07,254 --> 00:32:12,675
[SPEAKER_00]: See what is going on there, and whether you're not either holding on to something which is pleasurable there,

337
00:32:13,538 --> 00:32:16,839
[SPEAKER_00]: Or wanting to to change things on that level.

338
00:32:17,139 --> 00:32:17,339
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

339
00:32:17,399 --> 00:32:26,341
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in order to see that, like, again, everyone will start out and it will be like a novelty.

340
00:32:26,421 --> 00:32:29,642
[SPEAKER_02]: They will be able to see, oh, wow, I'm seeing my intentions now.

341
00:32:30,302 --> 00:32:34,323
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you don't remember that,

342
00:32:36,011 --> 00:32:42,953
[SPEAKER_02]: If you were to already see your intentions truly or fully, you would already have the right view, because you would have seen what craving is.

343
00:32:44,073 --> 00:32:53,635
[SPEAKER_02]: You can even think that, as watching your intentions as your new virtue and duty that you cling to, in a way that you're not even trying to improve it.

344
00:32:53,655 --> 00:32:57,816
[SPEAKER_02]: So to say, you're just assuming, okay, this is it, and I just keep doing it.

345
00:32:58,237 --> 00:33:03,978
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's why, in the end, it doesn't depend on... like, you know...

346
00:33:04,712 --> 00:33:20,255
[SPEAKER_02]: You might talk about meditation techniques being, you know, the obvious stuff, like focusing, absorption, breath observation, or what have you, but that's not what makes it wrong in and of itself.

347
00:33:20,275 --> 00:33:24,416
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, let's put it this way, that is the very coarse type of wrong.

348
00:33:25,376 --> 00:33:26,357
[SPEAKER_02]: But you can take anything.

349
00:33:26,657 --> 00:33:32,178
[SPEAKER_02]: You can take, again, even this that we're talking about now, this practice of watching your intentions,

350
00:33:34,402 --> 00:33:35,523
[SPEAKER_02]: In that same wrong way.

351
00:33:36,043 --> 00:33:46,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so somebody could say, I'm gonna sit down, let's say, or pause for a moment what I'm doing, what's my intentions, but all of that could come out of reacting to some unpleasant feeling which happens to be there.

352
00:33:46,952 --> 00:33:54,919
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, and that is self-mortification in a way, because you are doing it, because, again, this... and you're not questioning that.

353
00:33:55,259 --> 00:33:57,481
[SPEAKER_02]: You're kind of focused on, okay, what am I gonna do now?

354
00:33:57,521 --> 00:33:59,683
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm gonna question my intentions like this, blah, blah, blah.

355
00:34:00,245 --> 00:34:06,209
[SPEAKER_02]: But behind that, okay, there's that feeling, and right then and there, you're trying to get rid of it.

356
00:34:06,269 --> 00:34:11,733
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that is... that's kind of... going against yourself.

357
00:34:12,113 --> 00:34:12,293
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

358
00:34:12,394 --> 00:34:13,474
[SPEAKER_00]: It's self-mortification.

359
00:34:13,494 --> 00:34:16,016
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, but now suppose somebody recognizes that, right?

360
00:34:16,236 --> 00:34:25,603
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they were affected by some unpleasant feeling, they've been following what we are talking about just now, they say, okay, I pause for a minute, I'm going to check my intentions, then they recognize, aha,

361
00:34:26,163 --> 00:34:28,124
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just unpleasant feeling and sort of...

362
00:34:28,624 --> 00:34:32,566
[SPEAKER_00]: I was just reacting to that by now taking on this particular approach.

363
00:34:32,586 --> 00:34:32,686
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

364
00:34:32,986 --> 00:34:34,447
[SPEAKER_00]: So they include that.

365
00:34:36,007 --> 00:34:36,407
[SPEAKER_00]: Then what?

366
00:34:37,108 --> 00:34:39,229
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there anything more to be done than that?

367
00:34:39,929 --> 00:34:42,450
[SPEAKER_00]: Or are we saying that that is precisely what needs to be recognized?

368
00:34:47,501 --> 00:34:48,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, if you didn't pull it, that's it.

369
00:34:50,242 --> 00:34:53,204
[SPEAKER_00]: But you won't be able to do it once, and then, that's it.

370
00:34:53,584 --> 00:34:58,686
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it is not like, oh, now I've seen that I was reacting to this unpleasant feeling, great, let's just carry on.

371
00:34:58,886 --> 00:35:09,091
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's what I said just now, like, again, if you're taking the middle way as kind of a path that you walk once, and boom, you arrive, you arrive at the goal.

372
00:35:09,551 --> 00:35:09,731
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

373
00:35:10,573 --> 00:35:10,893
[SPEAKER_02]: But no.

374
00:35:11,053 --> 00:35:15,317
[SPEAKER_02]: Watching your intentions is the Noble Eightfold Path that you walk always.

375
00:35:15,397 --> 00:35:16,038
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it, yeah.

376
00:35:16,338 --> 00:35:19,101
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there's no end to it, in a sense.

377
00:35:19,121 --> 00:35:25,727
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, there is an end to it in the sense that when you fully develop it, there is no room for suffering to arise.

378
00:35:26,347 --> 00:35:28,129
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's not like you kind of...

379
00:35:29,466 --> 00:35:31,067
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, that's it.

380
00:35:31,127 --> 00:35:33,268
[SPEAKER_02]: I can forget about my intention now, because I caught it.

381
00:35:33,568 --> 00:35:34,349
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

382
00:35:34,609 --> 00:35:49,138
[SPEAKER_02]: It's more like, okay, you saw it now, but first and foremost, you see, well, precisely as the Buddha defined it, like, an ordinary person is one who doesn't even recognize their own mind.

383
00:35:50,278 --> 00:35:57,602
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can't assume that because you recognize it now, that at any moment, something else might come up.

384
00:35:58,203 --> 00:35:58,483
[SPEAKER_02]: So,

385
00:35:59,729 --> 00:36:06,932
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like you start searching for stuff, but you certainly don't rest in that recognition.

386
00:36:07,392 --> 00:36:09,833
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, you kind of take it further.

387
00:36:09,853 --> 00:36:17,216
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, so look, I have spent who knows how many months practicing, and I was completely unaware of this.

388
00:36:18,337 --> 00:36:22,898
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, or it's been happening many times, and only now I recognized it.

389
00:36:24,499 --> 00:36:26,660
[SPEAKER_02]: What tells me that there isn't

390
00:36:27,573 --> 00:36:39,279
[SPEAKER_02]: Ten million other things like these, exactly in the same principle, that right now, just like back then, I was not aware of, and now, like, I'm acting out of them still.

391
00:36:40,119 --> 00:36:48,223
[SPEAKER_02]: So, once you recognize that, instead of going in the kind of natural tendency of, okay, I found it, I found it, you recognize, wait, no, this is...

392
00:36:49,110 --> 00:36:52,811
[SPEAKER_02]: This is just like scraping the surface.

393
00:36:54,392 --> 00:37:14,219
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, there's no end to that, so to say, or at least you shouldn't be anticipating an end to it, because recognizing your intentions, in and of itself, is how you become safe, like how you prevent yourself from doing things that are against your own benefit.

394
00:37:16,977 --> 00:37:17,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

395
00:37:18,317 --> 00:37:24,099
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what you said earlier, wanting to recognize an end is, in a subtle sense, still wanting to get rid of things.

396
00:37:24,219 --> 00:37:24,639
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

397
00:37:24,679 --> 00:37:38,703
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you want an end to this, you're still hoping for a reward at the end.

398
00:37:38,863 --> 00:37:44,565
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, yes, there is freedom from suffering, there is nibbāna, there is all that, but that doesn't come

399
00:37:46,224 --> 00:37:48,766
[SPEAKER_02]: on the level that you expect, that you can crave for.

400
00:37:49,066 --> 00:37:49,246
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

401
00:37:49,287 --> 00:37:55,071
[SPEAKER_02]: Because whenever there is that kind of craving and expectation, that happens because of an underlying intention that you're ignoring.

402
00:37:55,111 --> 00:37:55,271
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

403
00:37:56,272 --> 00:37:56,953
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah.

404
00:37:56,973 --> 00:37:59,314
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not going to be your freedom from suffering.

405
00:37:59,334 --> 00:37:59,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

406
00:37:59,635 --> 00:38:01,036
[SPEAKER_02]: It's going to be freedom from suffering.

407
00:38:01,236 --> 00:38:01,436
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

408
00:38:01,756 --> 00:38:02,097
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

409
00:38:02,157 --> 00:38:03,638
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it is recognition of

410
00:38:04,578 --> 00:38:12,960
[SPEAKER_02]: Recognition of intentions, to the necessary extent, makes you unable to not see that those intentions are not for your benefit.

411
00:38:14,100 --> 00:38:21,221
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, once every single intention of that kind has been recognized, you can't act out of it, even if you want to.

412
00:38:21,781 --> 00:38:27,282
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it would have been obvious from the start, that this was not going to lead to any harm, to any good.

413
00:38:28,483 --> 00:38:29,943
[SPEAKER_02]: It was only because you didn't see it,

414
00:38:30,680 --> 00:38:31,742
[SPEAKER_02]: That you were acting out of it.

415
00:38:31,782 --> 00:38:34,286
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why the four noble truths go hand in hand.

416
00:38:34,406 --> 00:38:42,680
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't see this as suffering without also seeing this is the origin of suffering, this is the station of suffering, and this is the way to the station of suffering.

